<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/4th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Fei River</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/383">383</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Fei River was fought in 383 in present-day Anhui, where a much smaller Eastern Jin army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-xuan">Xie Xuan</a>, directed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xie-an">Xie An</a>, routed the invading force of Fu Jian of the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>. The defeat shattered Fu Jian's empire, which had unified northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>: subject peoples rebelled and founded Later Yan, Later Qin, Western Yan and other states, while the south remained independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-fei-river.jpg" alt="Battle of Fei River" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SY · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Fei_River.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When Fu Jian of the Former Qin invaded with a vast army in 383, Xie Shi and Xie Xuan, under the overall direction of Xie An, defeated its advance forces at the <b>Battle of Fei River</b>, and the Former Qin collapsed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/eastern-jin">Eastern Jin · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Fu Jiān's bid to unify China broke at the <b>Battle of Fei River</b>, Tuoba Gui, son or grandson of the last Prince of Dai, reasserted Tuoba independence in 386, first as Prince of Dai and then as Prince of Wei, whence the state's name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/northern-wei">Northern Wei · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the <b>Fei River</b> battle he fled to the Eastern Jin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-liang">Former Liang</a></li><li><blockquote>Fu Jian's invasion of the south ended in rout at the <b>Fei River</b> in 383 against the Eastern Jin.</blockquote><a href="/state/former-qin">Former Qin</a></li><li><blockquote>Returning east with the monk Kumarajiva and the spoils, he learned of the <b>Fei River</b> defeat, seized Guzang (Wuwei) in 386 and made himself ruler of the Hexi Corridor, later taking the title Heavenly King of Liang.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-liang-386-403">Later Liang (386-403)</a></li><li><blockquote>Yao Chang, a Qiang general of Former Qin, rebelled after the <b>Fei River</b> defeat in 383, captured and killed the Qin ruler Fu Jian in 385 and proclaimed himself emperor of Qin in 386, with his capital at Chang'an.</blockquote><a href="/state/later-qin">Later Qin</a></li></ul></section>
